const e=[{id:"sv-4-176",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`**Cost per resolved ticket (OMR)** was modelled on **team size X** (data from 5 to 45 agents) with a quadratic fit:

**Ŷ = 160 − 1.2X + 0.03X²**

At what team size is the predicted cost **lowest**?`,options:["20 agents","40 agents","−20 agents","25 agents"],answer:0,hint:"For $\\hat{Y}=a+bX+cX^2$ the turning point is at $X^{*}=\\dfrac{-b}{2c}$; c > 0 means the turning point is a minimum.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"a = 160, b = −1.2, c = 0.03, data range 5–45 agents. Because c > 0 the curve is U-shaped, so its turning point is a **minimum**."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$X^{*}=\\dfrac{-b}{2c}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$X^{*}=\\dfrac{-(-1.2)}{2\\times 0.03}=\\dfrac{1.2}{0.06}=20$$"},{title:"Decide / interpret",body:"20 agents is inside the 5–45 range, so it is a safe interpolation. The predicted cost there is $$\\hat{Y}=160-1.2(20)+0.03(20^2)=160-24+12=148$$ OMR per resolved ticket."}],final:"X* = 20 agents (cost ≈ 148 OMR) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Forgot the 2 in the denominator: 1.2 ÷ 0.03 = 40.","Dropped the minus sign in −b, so the negative slope gave −20, a team size that cannot exist.","Took the middle of the data range, (5 + 45) ÷ 2 = 25, instead of the turning point of the fitted curve."],trap:"The sign of the X² coefficient tells you the shape: c > 0 gives a minimum, c < 0 gives a maximum.",bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-177",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt fits a quadratic model of **video-call freeze rate (%)** on **network jitter (ms)**. Minitab's Sequential Analysis of Variance shows:

\`\`\`
Sequential Analysis of Variance
Source     DF      SS       F       P
Linear      1   540.0   48.21   0.000
Quadratic   1   135.0   22.73   0.000

Total SS = 900.0
\`\`\`

What share of the **total** variation in freeze rate does the **quadratic term add** on top of the straight line?`,options:["15.0%","25.0%","75.0%","37.5%"],answer:0,hint:"Extra share explained by a term = its sequential SS divided by the Total SS: $\\dfrac{SS_{term}}{SS_{Total}}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Sequential SS for the quadratic step = 135.0; Total SS = 900.0."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\Delta R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Quadratic}}{SS_{Total}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\Delta R^2=\\dfrac{135.0}{900.0}=0.15=15.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The straight line explains $\\dfrac{540}{900}=60\\%$; the X² term adds 15 points, so the quadratic model explains 75% in total. Its p = 0.000 says the extra bend is real."}],final:"ΔR² = 15.0% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Divided by the Linear SS (135 ÷ 540) instead of the Total SS.","This is R-sq of the whole quadratic model, (540 + 135) ÷ 900, not the extra share added by the X² term.","Divided by the variation left after the straight line (900 − 540 = 360), not by the Total SS."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-178",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`**Call set-up time (s)** was modelled on **signalling load (%)** with a cubic fit, n = 28, α = 0.05:

\`\`\`
Polynomial Regression Analysis: Setup_s versus Load_pct

Setup_s = 2.41 + 0.0315 Load_pct - 0.00142 Load_pct^2
          + 0.000011 Load_pct^3

S = 0.2184   R-Sq = 91.3%   R-Sq(adj) = 90.2%

Sequential Analysis of Variance
Source      DF        SS       F       P
Linear       1   10.4120   97.49   0.000
Quadratic    1    1.6215   35.09   0.000
Cubic        1    0.0105    0.22   0.643
\`\`\`

Which model order should the Black Belt use?`,options:["Cubic, because its R-Sq of 91.3% is the highest of the three fits","Quadratic: the quadratic step is significant (p = 0.000) but the cubic step adds almost nothing (p = 0.643)","Linear, because the linear step has the largest F-value","Cubic, because the model as a whole is significant"],answer:1,hint:"Read the Sequential ANOVA from the top: keep adding the next power only while its own P-Value is below α.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Sequential ANOVA tests each extra power **given the lower ones are already in**. The highest power with p < α sets the model order."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Linear p = 0.000 → keep. Quadratic p = 0.000 → keep. Cubic p = 0.643 > 0.05 → it adds only 0.0105 of SS, so drop it and refit as a quadratic."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"R-Sq always rises when a term is added, so the cubic’s R-Sq is not evidence. A large linear F only says the straight-line part matters, not that the curve does not."}],final:"Quadratic model → answer **B**",whyWrong:["R-Sq never falls when a term is added; the cubic step itself has p = 0.643, so the extra term is not worth keeping.","","The linear step’s large F does not make the significant quadratic step (p = 0.000) disappear.","An overall significant model can still contain a useless term; each step must be judged on its own P-Value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-179",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A fitted line plot of **cell throughput (Mbps)** against **active users** uses the quadratic model:

\`\`\`
Polynomial Regression Analysis: Throughput_Mbps versus Users

Throughput_Mbps = 12.0 + 4.60 Users - 0.110 Users^2

Model Summary
      S     R-sq   R-sq(adj)
 3.1460   84.7%      83.5%
\`\`\`

What percentage of the variation in throughput does this model explain?`,options:["83.5%","84.7%","92.0%","15.3%"],answer:1,hint:"R-sq = share of the variation in Y explained by the model; R-sq(adj) is the penalised version used to compare models.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The "% of variation explained" is **R-sq**. R-sq(adj) is a model-comparison statistic that penalises extra terms.'},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"R-sq = 84.7%, so the quadratic curve explains 84.7% of the variation in throughput. (The negative X² coefficient means the curve rises and then bends down.)"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"83.5% is R-sq(adj); 92.0% is $\\sqrt{0.847}$, a correlation-type number; 15.3% is the unexplained share."}],final:"R-sq = 84.7% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is R-sq(adj), used to compare models with different numbers of terms, not the share explained.","","Took the square root of R-sq (0.920), treating it like a correlation coefficient.","This is 100% − 84.7%, the share of variation the model does NOT explain."],trap:'R-sq vs R-sq(adj): "explained" → R-sq; "compare models" → R-sq(adj).',bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-180",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Three polynomial fits of **ticket backlog** on **staff absence (%)**, n = 22:

\`\`\`
Model        S      R-sq   R-sq(adj)  R-sq(pred)
Linear     4.812   71.6%     70.1%      66.2%
Quadratic  2.903   90.1%     89.0%      86.3%
Cubic      2.871   90.9%     89.3%      61.4%
\`\`\`

A colleague recommends the cubic model because its R-sq(adj) is the highest. Which value is the strongest warning against the cubic model?`,options:["The cubic R-sq(pred) of 61.4% is far below its R-sq of 90.9%, so the cubic curve is fitting noise and will predict new data poorly","The cubic R-sq(adj) of 89.3% is higher than the quadratic’s, which proves the cubic term is significant","The linear R-sq(pred) of 66.2% is higher than the cubic’s, so the linear model should be chosen","The cubic S of 2.871 is lower than the quadratic’s, which shows the cubic model is overfitted"],answer:0,hint:"R-sq(pred) shows how well the model predicts points it was not fitted on; a big drop from R-sq signals overfitting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq(pred) leaves each point out in turn and predicts it. If R-sq(pred) is much lower than R-sq, the model is chasing the noise in this particular data set."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Quadratic: 90.1% vs 86.3%, a small gap. Cubic: 90.9% vs 61.4%, a gap of almost 30 points. The cubic gains only 0.3 points of R-sq(adj) and 0.03 of S, yet predicts new data much worse."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A slightly higher R-sq(adj) is not a significance test, and a lower S is a good sign, not a sign of overfitting. The linear model is clearly worse than the quadratic on every row."}],final:"Cubic R-sq(pred) collapses → keep the quadratic → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A 0.3-point rise in R-sq(adj) does not prove significance; the cubic term’s own P-Value would be needed.","The right comparison is with the quadratic model, which beats the linear one on R-sq(adj), S and R-sq(pred).","A lower S is a sign of better fit, not of overfitting; the S change here is tiny either way."],trap:"R-sq(pred) far below R-sq = overfitted model.",bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-181",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`After fitting a straight line of **page-load time (s)** on **concurrent sessions**, a Black Belt groups the residuals by session band:

| Session band | Points | Mean residual (s) |
|---|---:|---:|
| Low (under 200) | 10 | +0.45 |
| Middle (200–600) | 12 | −0.75 |
| High (over 600) | 10 | +0.45 |

What does this pattern MOST likely show?`,options:["Non-constant variance (a funnel)","A missing curvature (quadratic) term","Residuals that depend on the time order","A single outlier in the middle band"],answer:1,hint:"Residuals should average about zero all along X; a +, −, + pattern across X is a U-shape.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a correct model, the residuals average about zero in every region of X. A run of positive, then negative, then positive means the straight line is too low at the ends and too high in the middle: the data curve."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"The pattern is +0.45, −0.75, +0.45, a U-shape. The residuals still sum to zero, as least squares requires: $10(0.45)+12(-0.75)+10(0.45)=4.5-9.0+4.5=0$. So a quadratic term is missing."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A funnel is about the **spread** of residuals, not their mean; time order is not in the table; a single outlier could not move the mean of 12 points so evenly."}],final:"U-shaped residual means → curvature → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A funnel shows a change in the spread of residuals; this table shows a change in their average.","","The table is grouped by sessions (X), not by time order, so it cannot show dependence over time.","One outlier would not give a balanced +, −, + pattern across 32 points."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-182",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A scatter plot of **data-bundle uptake (%)** against **price discount (%)** rises slowly at small discounts, climbs steeply in the middle and then **levels off again** at large discounts — two bends. The Black Belt wants one polynomial in discount. Which model form is the MOST suitable starting point?",options:["Cubic: Y = a + bX + cX² + dX³","Quadratic: Y = a + bX + cX²","Linear: Y = a + bX","Multiple regression with discount and price as two separate X’s"],answer:0,hint:"Linear = no bend; quadratic = one bend; cubic = up to two bends.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The order of a polynomial sets how many bends it can follow: a quadratic has one, a cubic up to two."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Slow rise → steep climb → level off is an S-shape with two bends, so start with the cubic model and then check that the X³ term is significant."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A straight line has no bend and a quadratic only one. Discount and price describe the same thing, so using both as X’s would add multicollinearity, not curvature."}],final:"Two bends → cubic → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A quadratic has only one bend, so it cannot follow an S-shape.","A straight line cannot follow any bend, let alone two.","Price and discount carry the same information; two collinear X’s do not create a curve."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-183",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A linear model of **fibre-splice loss (dB)** on **splice temperature** has R-sq = 52%. The scatter plot hinted at a slight bend, so the Black Belt fits a quadratic. The quadratic term has **p = 0.41**, R-sq(adj) **falls** from 50.3% to 49.1% and S rises. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Keep the linear model and look for other X’s (for example technician or fibre batch) with multiple regression, because temperature alone explains only about half the variation","Try a cubic model, because the quadratic was not curved enough","Drop temperature completely, because an R-sq of 52% means it has no effect","Keep the quadratic model, because its R-sq is slightly higher"],answer:0,hint:"Keep a curve only if its term is significant, R-sq(adj) rises and S falls; if one X explains too little, add X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book uses the curved model only if R-sq improves meaningfully or the residuals improve. When R-sq is not high enough, it turns to **multiple** regression."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"p = 0.41, falling R-sq(adj) and rising S all say the bend is not real, so keep the straight line. An R-sq of 52% leaves almost half the variation unexplained, which points to other inputs."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A cubic adds even more terms to a curve that is not there. R-sq = 52% shows that temperature does have a real effect, so it should stay."}],final:"Keep linear and search for more X’s → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The quadratic term was not significant, so a higher-order curve is even less justified.","Temperature explains about half of the variation, which is a real and useful effect.","R-sq always rises a little when a term is added; R-sq(adj) and S both got worse."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-184",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In a quadratic model of **smartphone battery drain (%/h)** on **screen brightness**, Minitab shows Brightness **p = 0.14** and Brightness² **p = 0.001**. A colleague proposes removing the linear Brightness term because it is not significant, which would leave Y = a + cX². What should the Black Belt do?",options:["Keep the linear term: a model with an X² term should keep the lower-order X term (a hierarchical model), even if that term alone is not significant","Remove the X² term instead, because a curve can only be kept when its linear term is significant","Remove the linear term, because every term with p > 0.05 must be removed","Replace both terms with a single cubic term, which has the best chance of being significant"],answer:0,hint:"Model hierarchy: if a higher power of X is in the model, all lower powers of X stay in.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Polynomial models are kept **hierarchical**: if X² is significant, X stays in. Without it the curve is forced to have its turning point at X = 0, which is a strong assumption with no process reason behind it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Brightness² (p = 0.001) shows a real bend, so the quadratic stays and the linear term is kept with it. Minitab’s Fitted Line Plot always fits the full Y = a + bX + cX²."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'The "remove p > 0.05" rule is for separate predictors in multiple regression, not for the lower power of a significant polynomial. Dropping the significant X² would throw away the curvature.'}],final:"Keep both X and X² → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The X² term is the significant one (p = 0.001); removing it throws away the real curvature.","The p > 0.05 removal rule does not apply to the lower-order term of a significant polynomial term.","A cubic term alone is not a hierarchical model and has no evidence behind it."],trap:"Hierarchy: keep X whenever X² is in the model.",bookRef:"Book pp. 449–456"},{id:"sv-4-185",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A fitted line plot of **site-visit time** on **distance travelled** looks curved, but the bend comes from **one job** at the far right, which Minitab flags with a large standardized residual (**R**). All the other points lie close to a straight line. What should the Black Belt do FIRST?",options:["Investigate that job (data entry, special cause) before deciding whether a curved model is needed","Fit a cubic model so that the curve passes through the unusual job","Delete the job, because it lowers R-sq","Switch to a quadratic model, because any R flag proves curvature"],answer:0,hint:"An R flag is a signal to investigate the point; curvature must show as a pattern across many points, not one.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists R-flagged points as one possible **hint** of curvature. A hint is not proof, so check the point before you change the model."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'Every other point lies on a straight line, so the "curve" depends on one job. Check its record first: wrong distance, wrong time or a special cause such as a road closure.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Bending the model to fit one point overfits it, and deleting a point only to raise R-sq is never acceptable."}],final:"Investigate the flagged job first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Bending the model to pass through one point is overfitting; the other points show a straight line.","Points are never deleted just to raise R-sq; the cause must be found first.","An R flag can hint at curvature, but one point is not a pattern."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-186",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A Black Belt adds an X² term to a straight-line model and R-sq rises from 76.0% to 76.3%. Why is this rise, **on its own**, NOT evidence that the curve is real?",options:["Because R-sq can only be used for multiple regression, not for curves","Because a rise of less than 1% always means the new term is significant","Because R-sq never decreases when a term is added, even a useless one","Because R-sq falls whenever the relationship is truly curved"],answer:2,hint:"Adding any term can never lower R-sq; use the term’s P-Value, R-sq(adj) and S instead.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Least squares with an extra term can always fit at least as well as without it, so R-sq can only rise or stay the same."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 0.3-point rise is what even a useless X² term would give. To judge the curve, look at the quadratic term’s P-Value, R-sq(adj) and S."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"R-sq is used for all regression models. A small rise says nothing about significance, and R-sq rises (it does not fall) when a real curve is captured."}],final:"R-sq never falls when a term is added → answer **C**",whyWrong:["R-sq is reported for simple, polynomial and multiple regression alike.","The size of the R-sq rise is not a significance test; the term’s own P-Value is.","","When a real curve is captured, R-sq rises noticeably; it does not fall."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-187",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book lists the exponential form **Y = a·bˣ**. A model of **cumulative app downloads** gives **Ŷ = 5,000 × 1.08ˣ**, with X in months. What does b = 1.08 mean?",options:["Each extra month multiplies predicted downloads by 1.08, a growth of about 8% per month","Downloads grow by 108% each month","The curve has one bend at X = 1.08 months","Each extra month adds 1.08 downloads"],answer:0,hint:"In Y = a·bˣ, a one-unit rise in X multiplies Y by b (growth rate = b − 1).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In an exponential model X is an exponent, so each extra unit of X **multiplies** Y by b. Y does not go up by a fixed amount."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"From month X to X + 1, downloads go from $5000\\times 1.08^{X}$ to $5000\\times 1.08^{X+1}$, which is 1.08 times as many: an 8% rise each month."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Adding a fixed amount per month describes a straight line. "108% growth" would mean b = 2.08. Exponential curves have no single bend point.'}],final:"b = 1.08 → ×1.08 per month (≈ 8% growth) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Mixed up the multiplier with the growth rate: 108% growth would need b = 2.08.","b is a growth multiplier, not the location of a bend; exponential curves bend smoothly everywhere.","Adding a constant per month describes a linear model, not an exponential one."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-188",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following support choosing a **quadratic** model over a straight line for the same data **EXCEPT**:",options:["The quadratic term has p = 0.004 in the Sequential ANOVA","S falls from 3.8 to 2.6","R-sq rises from 81.0% to 81.2% while R-sq(adj) falls","The U-shape in the residuals-versus-fits plot disappears"],answer:2,hint:"Evidence for a curve: significant X² term, higher R-sq(adj), lower S, cleaner residuals.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book keeps the curved model only if it improves the fit significantly or the residuals behave better."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"p = 0.004, a lower S and a vanished U-shape all support the curve. A 0.2-point rise in R-sq with a **falling** R-sq(adj) says the extra term does not earn its place."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:"The R-sq / R-sq(adj) option is the only one that argues against the quadratic."}],final:"Falling R-sq(adj) does not support the curve → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A significant quadratic term (p < 0.05) is direct evidence for the curve.","A smaller S means smaller prediction errors, which supports the curve.","","Removing the U-shaped residual pattern shows the curve fixed the misfit."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–453"},{id:"sv-4-189",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, if adding a quadratic term makes **R-sq(adj) fall** and **S rise**, the simpler linear model should be kept. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Simpler is better when the extra term adds nothing: compare R-sq(adj) and S, not R-sq.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'R-sq always rises with an extra term, so the book compares R-sq(adj) and S. In its Payton example, R-sq(adj) fell and S rose, and the decision was "stick with the linear model".'},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"Falling R-sq(adj) and rising S mean the X² term costs more than it explains, so the statement is true."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book’s own counter-example keeps the linear model exactly when R-sq(adj) falls and S rises."],bookRef:"Book pp. 449–456"},{id:"sv-4-190",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"The ANOVA table of a 3-predictor model of **weekly SLA breach minutes** (n = 24) shows **SS Regression = 420** and **SS Error = 180**. What is R-sq?",options:["30.0%","65.5%","70.0%","42.9%"],answer:2,hint:"$R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Regression}}{SS_{Total}}$, with $SS_{Total}=SS_{Regression}+SS_{Error}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"SS Regression = 420, SS Error = 180, so SS Total = 420 + 180 = 600."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Regression}}{SS_{Total}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$R^2=\\dfrac{420}{600}=0.700=70.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The three X’s together explain 70% of the variation in breach minutes. R-sq(adj) would be lower (65.5%) because it penalises the three predictors."}],final:"R-sq = 70.0% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Used SS Error in the numerator (180 ÷ 600): this is the unexplained share.","This is R-sq(adj), 1 − 0.30 × 23 ÷ 20; the question asks for R-sq.","","Divided SS Error by SS Regression (180 ÷ 420) instead of using SS Total as the denominator."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-191",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A field-repair model is

**Repair_h = 1.20 + 0.30 Faults − 0.45 Crew + 0.020 Distance_km**

Next month the planner expects each job to have **4 more faults** and **1 more crew member**, with the same distance. By how much does the predicted repair time per job change?`,options:["+0.75 h","+1.95 h","+1.20 h","+1.65 h"],answer:0,hint:"Change in prediction = sum of (partial coefficient × change in that X); the constant cancels out: $\\Delta\\hat{Y}=b_1\\Delta X_1+b_2\\Delta X_2$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"ΔFaults = +4 (b = 0.30), ΔCrew = +1 (b = −0.45), ΔDistance = 0."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\Delta\\hat{Y}=b_{Faults}\\Delta X_{Faults}+b_{Crew}\\Delta X_{Crew}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\Delta\\hat{Y}=0.30\\times 4+(-0.45)\\times 1=1.20-0.45=0.75$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Predicted repair time rises by 0.75 h per job. Each coefficient is a partial effect, so the two changes simply add up, and the constant cancels."}],final:"ΔŶ = +0.75 h → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Added the constant 1.20 to the change; the constant cancels when you compare two predictions.","Ignored the extra crew member and counted only the faults.","Ignored the minus sign of the Crew coefficient: 1.20 + 0.45."],trap:"Negative coefficients: keep the sign when you substitute.",bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-192",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt fits **8 predictors** to **15 months** of billing-dispute data:

\`\`\`
Model Summary
      S     R-sq   R-sq(adj)   R-sq(pred)
 1.2047   91.0%      79.0%        12.5%
\`\`\`

Which conclusion follows the book's model-selection guidelines?`,options:["R-sq and R-sq(adj) are 12 points apart and R-sq(pred) is very low: the model has too many terms for the data and should be reduced","The model is excellent because R-sq is above 90%","R-sq(adj) of 79.0% shows that 79% of the variation is caused by the eight X’s","The model is acceptable because S is small"],answer:0,hint:"Book guideline: R-sq and R-sq(adj) should be close (well under 10 points apart); a big gap means too many terms.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq(adj) penalises each predictor. With many X’s and few rows, R-sq is inflated and R-sq(adj) falls well below it. R-sq(pred) then shows how badly the model predicts new data."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"There are 8 X’s for only 15 rows. R-sq is 91.0% but R-sq(adj) is only 79.0%, a 12-point gap, and R-sq(pred) is only 12.5%. The model is fitting noise. Use Best Subsets to cut it down to a few useful X’s."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A high R-sq is exactly what overfitting produces. Regression shows association, not causation. S is in the units of Y and cannot rescue an overfitted model."}],final:"Too many terms → reduce the model → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A high R-sq with many terms and few rows is the typical sign of overfitting, not of a good model.","Regression on historical data shows association, not cause, and the gap shows the number itself is unreliable.","S alone cannot show overfitting; the R-sq / R-sq(adj) / R-sq(pred) gaps do."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-193",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Stepwise regression of **daily repeat-call volume**, α to enter = α to remove = 0.15:

\`\`\`
Stepwise Selection of Terms
Candidate terms: Calls, Agents, Tenure, Outages, Weekend

          Step 1        Step 2        Step 3        Step 4        Step 5
          Coef    P     Coef    P     Coef    P     Coef    P     Coef    P
Constant  41.2          35.6          30.1          31.0          32.4
Calls    0.218 0.000   0.201 0.000   0.188 0.000   0.190 0.000   0.193 0.000
Outages                2.94  0.003   2.61  0.006   2.66  0.005   2.71  0.004
Tenure                              -0.41  0.094  -0.29  0.188
Agents                                            -1.21  0.027  -1.33  0.012

S         6.12          5.31          5.02          4.90          4.93
R-sq     58.4%         69.2%         72.9%         74.9%         74.6%
\`\`\`

Which terms are in the **final** model?`,options:["Calls and Outages only","Calls, Outages and Agents","Calls, Outages, Tenure and Agents","All five candidate terms"],answer:1,hint:"Stepwise adds the best term with p < α-to-enter and removes any term whose p rises above α-to-remove; the last step is the final model.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stepwise builds the model in steps. At each step it may add a term (p < α to enter) or remove one that has become weak (p > α to remove). The **last column** is the final model."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Step 3 adds Tenure (p = 0.094 < 0.15). Step 4 adds Agents, and Tenure’s p rises to 0.188 > 0.15. Step 5 removes Tenure. Weekend never enters. Final model: Calls, Outages, Agents."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Step 4 is not the end, and terms with p between 0.05 and 0.15 can enter with α = 0.15. Weekend never appears in any step."}],final:"Final = Calls, Outages, Agents → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is the Step 2 model; Agents entered later with p = 0.012.","","This is the Step 4 model; Tenure was removed at Step 5 because its p rose to 0.188 > 0.15.","Weekend never entered, and Tenure was removed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-194",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Multiple regression of **fibre-build time (days)**, n = 32:

\`\`\`
Coefficients
Term           Coef   SE Coef   T-Value   P-Value    VIF
Constant       4.85      1.10      4.41     0.000
Distance_km  0.0410    0.0062      6.61     0.000    1.2
Splices       0.380     0.095      4.00     0.000    1.3
Permit_days   2.150     0.810      2.65     0.013    1.1
\`\`\`

Which predictor shows the **strongest statistical evidence** of a relationship with build time?`,options:["Permit_days, because it has the largest coefficient","Splices, because its VIF is the highest","Distance_km, because it has the largest T-Value (smallest P-Value), even though its coefficient is the smallest","All three equally, because all their P-Values are below 0.05"],answer:2,hint:"Strength of evidence = |T-Value| = Coef ÷ SE Coef; coefficient size depends on the units of X.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A coefficient’s size depends on the units of its X (km, splices, days), so sizes cannot be compared. The evidence that a term matters is its T-Value, the coefficient divided by its standard error."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Distance: $\\dfrac{0.0410}{0.0062}=6.61$, the largest |T| and the smallest P-Value. Permit_days has the biggest coefficient but the weakest evidence: $\\dfrac{2.150}{0.810}=2.65$."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'VIF measures how strongly the X’s are correlated with each other, not how strong the evidence is. "All below 0.05" makes all three significant, but not equally strong.'}],final:"Distance_km has the largest |T| → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A large coefficient can come from the units of X (days vs km); its T-Value of 2.65 is the weakest.","VIF measures correlation among the X’s, not the strength of a term’s link with Y.","","All three are significant, but their T-Values (6.61, 4.00, 2.65) show different strengths of evidence."],trap:"Largest coefficient ≠ most important term.",bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-195",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Model of **monthly care-call volume (thousands)**, n = 30, α = 0.05:

\`\`\`
Analysis of Variance
Source       DF   Adj SS   Adj MS   F-Value   P-Value
Regression    3    812.4    270.8     19.62     0.000
Error        26    358.9     13.8
Total        29   1171.3

Coefficients
Term           Coef   SE Coef   T-Value   P-Value    VIF
Constant       6.20      3.05      2.03     0.052
Subscribers  0.0120    0.0081      1.48     0.150   14.6
Active_SIMs  0.0105    0.0079      1.33     0.195   15.2
Data_GB      0.0310    0.0190      1.63     0.115    2.1
\`\`\`

What is the MOST likely explanation of this output?`,options:["Subscribers and Active_SIMs carry almost the same information (VIF about 15), which inflates their standard errors; remove one of them and refit","None of the X’s is related to call volume, so the model should be abandoned","The F-test must be wrong, because it disagrees with the individual P-Values","Data_GB should be removed first, because it has the lowest VIF"],answer:0,hint:"Significant overall F but no significant single term + high VIFs = multicollinearity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When two X’s move together, the model cannot tell their effects apart. Their standard errors become inflated, so each one looks non-significant even though together they clearly explain Y."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Overall p = 0.000 (R-sq ≈ 69%), but no term has p < 0.05. Subscribers and Active_SIMs have VIFs of 14.6 and 15.2, well above 5–10. Drop the one that is less useful or harder to measure, refit, and re-check the P-Values."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The X’s clearly explain call volume (F p = 0.000). The F-test is not wrong, because collinearity is exactly what makes the two tests disagree. Data_GB has a healthy VIF and is not the cause."}],final:"Multicollinearity between Subscribers and Active_SIMs → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The overall F-test (p = 0.000) shows the X’s together are clearly related to call volume.","Both tests are correct: collinearity makes the joint effect significant while each separate effect looks weak.","Data_GB has a low VIF (2.1) and is not the source of the problem; the collinear pair is."],trap:"Overall F significant + all terms non-significant + VIF > 10 = multicollinearity.",bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-196",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Model of **incident restore time (h)** with a categorical predictor, n = 40:

\`\`\`
Regression Equation
Region
North   Restore_h = 3.10 + 0.0820 Backlog
South   Restore_h = 5.45 + 0.0820 Backlog

Coefficients
Term        Coef   SE Coef   T-Value   P-Value
Constant    3.10      0.42      7.38     0.000
Backlog   0.0820    0.0110      7.45     0.000
Region
  South     2.35      0.51      4.61     0.000
\`\`\`

What does the coefficient **2.35** for Region South mean?`,options:["South incidents take 2.35 times as long to restore as North incidents","Every South incident has a restore time of 2.35 h","Each South incident adds 2.35 tickets to the backlog","At the same backlog, South incidents take on average 2.35 h longer to restore than North incidents (the reference level)"],answer:3,hint:"An indicator (0/1) coefficient = average shift in Y for that level versus the reference level, other X’s held constant.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A categorical X enters as indicator variables. Each coefficient is the shift in Y for that level compared with the **reference level** (here North), with the other X’s held constant."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The two equations differ only in the constant: 5.45 − 3.10 = 2.35. At any backlog, South restores take 2.35 h longer, and the difference is significant (p = 0.000)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The shift is additive (hours), not a ratio. It is not a full restore time, and it does not act on the backlog."}],final:"South = +2.35 h versus North at the same backlog → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The model adds 2.35 h; it does not multiply the restore time.","Restore time also depends on backlog; 2.35 h is only the difference from North.","The coefficient changes restore time (Y), not the backlog (an X).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-197",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Part of a regression of **monthly billing complaints**, n = 36:

\`\`\`
Analysis of Variance
Source       DF   Adj SS   Adj MS   F-Value   P-Value
Regression    2   1520.6    760.3     21.47     0.000
Error        33   1168.6     35.4
Total        35   2689.2

Coefficients
Term        Coef   SE Coef   T-Value   P-Value
Constant    4.10      3.25      1.26     0.216
\`\`\`

Which value tests H₀: **all slope coefficients are zero** (the model as a whole is useless)?`,options:["The P-Value of the Constant (0.216)","The Adj SS of the Error (1168.6)","The P-Value on the Regression line of the ANOVA table (0.000)","The F-Value divided by the Error DF"],answer:2,hint:"The ANOVA table’s Regression row (F and P) tests whether the X’s together explain Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The overall F-test in the ANOVA table tests H₀: β₁ = β₂ = … = 0. If its p < α, at least one X is useful."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Regression F = 21.47 with p = 0.000 < 0.05, so reject H₀: the model explains real variation in complaints. Then look at each term’s own P-Value."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Constant’s P-Value tests whether the intercept is zero, which says nothing about the X’s. SS Error is an input to the test, not the test itself."}],final:"Regression row P = 0.000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Constant’s P-Value tests only whether the intercept is zero, not the X’s.","SS Error is the unexplained variation; it feeds into F but is not a test result.","","F divided by DF has no meaning; F is compared with the F-distribution, which gives the P-Value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-198",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Best Subsets output for **dropped-call rate (%)**, n = 30 (candidates: Load, Handover failures, Rain, Site age, Interference):

\`\`\`
                     R-Sq   Mallows
Vars   R-Sq   (adj)      Cp       S   Predictors
   1   48.2    46.4    41.3   0.512   Load
   2   71.5    69.4    12.6   0.385   Load Handover
   3   79.8    77.5     3.1   0.330   Load Handover Interf
   3   73.0    69.9    10.9   0.382   Load Handover Rain
   4   80.4    77.3     4.4   0.332   Load Handover Age Interf
   5   80.6    76.6     6.0   0.336   All five
\`\`\`

Which model best meets the book's selection guidelines?`,options:["The 5-variable model, because it has the highest R-Sq","The 3-variable model with Load, Handover and Interference","The 4-variable model, because its R-Sq is higher than the 3-variable model’s","The 2-variable model, because it has the largest jump in R-Sq"],answer:1,hint:"Choose high R-Sq(adj), small Mallows Cp (below the number of terms), small S, and the fewest X’s when the fit is nearly the same.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s guidelines: R-Sq(adj) high and close to R-Sq, Mallows Cp small and less than the number of terms, S small, and the simpler model when the fit is about the same."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The Load–Handover–Interference model has the highest R-Sq(adj) (77.5%), the lowest S (0.330) and Cp = 3.1, which is below its 4 terms (3 X’s plus the constant). Adding Age or Rain lowers R-Sq(adj)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"R-Sq always rises as X’s are added, so the 5-X row wins on R-Sq automatically. The 2-X model has Cp = 12.6, far above its terms, which means important X’s are missing."}],final:"3-variable model (Load, Handover, Interf) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["R-Sq always rises with more X’s; R-Sq(adj) actually falls to 76.6% and S rises.","","Its R-Sq is only 0.6 points higher, while R-Sq(adj) falls and S rises; the extra X does not earn its place.","Cp = 12.6 is far above its 3 terms, so this model is missing an important X."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-199",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt adds predictors one at a time to a model of **order-fallout rate** (n = 25). Each row adds the next-best X:

| X’s in model | R-sq | R-sq(adj) |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 52.0% | 49.9% |
| 2 | 66.0% | 62.9% |
| 3 | 72.0% | 68.0% |
| 4 | 73.0% | 67.6% |
| 5 | 73.5% | 66.5% |

How many predictors should she keep?`,options:["5, because R-sq is highest there","4, because R-sq still rises by a full point","2, because the biggest single jump in R-sq happens there","3, because R-sq(adj) peaks there and falls after"],answer:3,hint:"R-sq always rises; R-sq(adj) rises only while a new X adds more than its penalty. Stop where R-sq(adj) peaks.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq(adj) adds a penalty for each predictor, so it falls once a new X explains less than chance alone would."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"R-sq(adj): 49.9 → 62.9 → **68.0** → 67.6 → 66.5. It peaks at 3 X’s. The 4th and 5th predictors raise R-sq by only 1.0 and 0.5 points, less than their penalty."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Picking by R-sq always picks the largest model. The biggest jump only tells you which X matters most, not where to stop."}],final:"3 predictors (R-sq(adj) peak) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["R-sq always rises when an X is added, so the highest R-sq is always the largest model.","A one-point R-sq gain is less than the penalty for the extra X: R-sq(adj) falls from 68.0% to 67.6%.","The third X still raises R-sq(adj) from 62.9% to 68.0%, so stopping at 2 leaves a useful X out.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-200",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Marketing wants to model **monthly ARPU (OMR)** using **tenure (months)** and **contract type** (prepaid / postpaid) **together**, from 400 customer records. Which approach fits?",options:["Multiple linear regression with tenure as a continuous X and contract type coded as an indicator (0/1) variable","A chi-square test of contract type against ARPU band","A one-way ANOVA of ARPU by tenure","A simple linear regression of ARPU on contract type only"],answer:0,hint:"Continuous Y with several X’s (continuous and/or categorical) → multiple regression; categorical X’s go in as indicator variables.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multiple regression handles one continuous Y and several X’s at once. A two-level categorical X is coded 0/1 (an indicator variable)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"ARPU is continuous, tenure is continuous and contract type has two levels, so fit ARPU = b₀ + b₁·Tenure + b₂·Postpaid. b₂ is the ARPU difference at the same tenure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chi-square needs two categorical variables. One-way ANOVA needs a categorical X, but tenure is continuous. One X alone ignores tenure."}],final:"MLR with an indicator for contract type → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Chi-square compares two categorical variables; it would throw away the continuous ARPU and tenure data.","One-way ANOVA needs a categorical factor; tenure is continuous, and contract type would be left out.","Using only contract type ignores tenure, which the team wants to include at the same time."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-201",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt wants to predict whether each customer **churns (yes/no)** from tenure, outage minutes and complaint count. A colleague suggests multiple linear regression. What is the issue?",options:["None — multiple linear regression handles any kind of Y","Multiple linear regression cannot use more than two X’s","Multiple linear regression needs a continuous Y; a yes/no outcome needs a different technique, such as binary logistic regression","Multiple linear regression needs the X’s to be yes/no as well"],answer:2,hint:"MLR: continuous Y, any mix of continuous and indicator X’s. Binary Y → logistic regression.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multiple linear regression models a **continuous** Y with normally distributed residuals. A yes/no Y breaks this: predictions can fall below 0 or above 1, and the residuals cannot be normal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Churn is a binary Y, so model the probability of churn with binary logistic regression (a Black Belt-level tool). Or turn it into a continuous measure, such as churn rate per segment."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"MLR takes any number of X’s, and its X’s can be continuous. The problem is the type of Y."}],final:"Binary Y → not MLR → answer **C**",whyWrong:["MLR assumes a continuous Y; a 0/1 Y gives impossible predictions and non-normal residuals.","MLR can use many X’s; the limit is the amount of data, not the number two.","","X’s in MLR can be continuous or categorical; the issue is that Y is binary."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-202",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In two years of historical data, **training hours** and **agent tenure** always rose together. An MLR of **first-contact resolution** shows both with large VIFs, and management wants to know which one really drives FCR. What is the BEST way to separate their effects?",options:["Run Best Subsets again with a larger α","Keep both X’s and read their coefficients as separate effects","Remove the constant term so that both slopes become significant","Run a designed experiment (or controlled pilot) that varies training hours and tenure independently"],answer:3,hint:"Historical regression cannot separate X’s that always moved together; only a DOE that sets the X’s independently can.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"MLR uses passive historical data. If two X’s were never varied separately, no analysis of that data can split their effects. The book says MLR is not experimentation and does not replace a DOE."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Large VIFs confirm that training and tenure are confounded. Give extra training to some new and some experienced agents (a balanced design) to see each effect alone."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Re-running Best Subsets on the same data cannot create information that is missing. The coefficients of collinear X’s are unstable, and dropping the constant only distorts the model."}],final:"DOE / controlled pilot → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Best Subsets on the same confounded data cannot separate effects that never varied independently.","With high VIFs the separate coefficients are unstable and cannot be trusted as individual effects.","Removing the constant biases the model; it does not fix collinearity.",""],trap:"Only a designed experiment proves cause and effect; MLR shows association.",bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-203",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"One X in a model of **fibre-install time** is **site difficulty**, scored by surveyors. Its Gage R&R gave **45% study variation**. What effect will this most likely have on the multiple regression?",options:["None — measurement error in an X has no effect on regression","It adds to the model error, lowering R-sq and the confidence in the coefficients","It raises R-sq, because more variation is available to explain","It affects only the normal probability plot of the residuals"],answer:1,hint:"In MLR the model error includes the measurement error of every input.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the measurement error of each X adds to the model error in MLR. A noisy X lowers R-sq and widens the confidence in its coefficient."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"45% study variation is far above the 30% limit, so the difficulty score is mostly noise. Its real effect will be weakened and blurred. Fix the scoring (clear definitions, surveyor training) before trusting the model."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Noise cannot be explained, so R-sq falls, not rises. The damage shows up in the coefficients and S, not only in one residual plot."}],final:"Noisy X → more model error, lower R-sq → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book states that measurement error in the X’s adds to model error.","","Random measurement noise cannot be explained by the model, so it lowers R-sq.","The effect is on R-sq, S and the coefficients, not just on residual normality."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-204",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The team believes the effect of **overtime hours** on **order-entry error rate** is much larger for **new agents** than for experienced ones. Why will a basic MLR model, Y = b₀ + b₁·Overtime + b₂·Tenure, not capture this?",options:["Because MLR cannot include more than one X","Because the overtime coefficient will always be zero","Because the basic model gives Overtime one slope at every tenure; the effect the team describes is an interaction, which a basic MLR does not include, so a DOE (or an added interaction term) is needed","Because tenure must be removed whenever overtime is in the model"],answer:2,hint:"If the effect of one X depends on the level of another X, that is an interaction, and a basic additive MLR model does not include it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In a basic MLR model each X has one partial slope, the same at every level of the other X’s. An effect that **depends** on another X is an interaction. The book notes that interactions are not part of basic MLR."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Overtime having a bigger effect on new agents is an Overtime × Tenure interaction. A DOE estimates interactions directly. In regression you would add an Overtime×Tenure term."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"MLR handles several X’s. Nothing forces the overtime slope to be zero, and the two X’s can sit in the same model."}],final:"Interaction → not in basic MLR → answer **C**",whyWrong:["MLR is designed for two or more X’s.","The overtime slope can be any value; it is just the same at all tenures.","","Both X’s can be in the model together; what is missing is their interaction."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-205",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A final MLR model built on last year’s data predicts an average **6.0 h** provisioning time for this month’s order mix, with a 95% CI of (5.6, 6.4) h. The actual average from current data is **9.2 h**. What is the BEST next step?",options:["Widen the interval to 99% so that 9.2 h falls inside it","Use the model anyway, because it had R-sq = 88% when it was built","Delete this month’s data as an outlier","Investigate what has changed in the process (new X’s or shifted conditions); the model failed verification against current data and should not be used until it is rebuilt"],answer:3,hint:"Step 6 of the MLR roadmap: verify the model with current process data; a model that fails verification is not used.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The last step of the book’s MLR roadmap is to **verify the model with current process data** to confirm the model error. A historical fit is only useful if it still describes today’s process."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"9.2 h is far outside (5.6, 6.4). Something important has changed, such as a new system, a new order type or a missing X. Find it, then rebuild and re-verify the model."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Changing the confidence level to fit the result hides the problem. An old R-sq says nothing about today’s process. A whole month of current data is not an outlier."}],final:"Verification failed → investigate and rebuild → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Choosing the confidence level after seeing the result just hides a real change in the process.","R-sq describes last year’s fit; it cannot show that the model still applies now.","A whole month of current data is evidence of change, not an outlier to delete.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-206",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A model of **ticket backlog** must include **Vendor**, which has three levels (A, B, C). How should Vendor enter the regression?",options:["As one column coded 1, 2, 3 and treated as a continuous X","As two indicator (0/1) variables, with the third vendor as the reference level","As three indicator variables plus the constant","It cannot be included: categorical X’s are not allowed in regression"],answer:1,hint:"A categorical X with k levels enters as k − 1 indicator variables; the missing level is the reference.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Codes 1, 2, 3 have no real order or distance, so they cannot be used as a continuous X. Each level except one becomes a 0/1 column, and the left-out level is the **reference**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With 3 vendors you need 2 indicators, for example B and C, with A as the reference. Their coefficients show how much higher or lower backlog is than with A, other X’s held constant. Minitab does this when Vendor is entered as a categorical predictor."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Three indicators plus the constant are perfectly collinear (they always sum to 1). Categorical X’s are allowed in regression."}],final:"k − 1 = 2 indicators → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Codes 1–3 would force equal steps A→B→C, which have no meaning for vendor labels.","","Three indicators plus a constant are an exact linear combination (they always sum to 1), so the model cannot be estimated.","Categorical X’s are allowed when coded as indicator variables."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-207",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Minitab prints: *“The following terms cannot be estimated and were removed: Total_calls.”* The model also contains **Inbound_calls** and **Outbound_calls**. Why was Total_calls removed?",options:["Total_calls had a P-Value above 0.05","Total_calls had missing values in some rows","Total_calls had too small a coefficient","Total_calls = Inbound + Outbound, so it is an exact linear combination of other X’s (perfect multicollinearity)"],answer:3,hint:"If one X is an exact sum or multiple of other X’s, its effect cannot be separated, so it cannot be estimated.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When one X is an exact linear combination of others, there are endless coefficient sets that fit equally well. Minitab cannot estimate it and drops it. This is the extreme case of multicollinearity, with an infinite VIF."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Total = Inbound + Outbound, so it adds no information. Keep either the total or the two parts, not all three."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"P-Values and coefficient sizes do not stop a term from being estimated. Missing values drop rows, not terms."}],final:"Exact linear combination → cannot be estimated → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A non-significant term is still estimated and printed; it is not removed automatically.","Missing values cause rows to be dropped, not a whole term.","A small coefficient is still estimated; size is not a reason for removal.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-208",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"In which situation is multiple regression on historical data the MORE practical choice than a DOE?",options:["When the X’s can be set freely at chosen levels and are cheap to change","When the X’s (for example rainfall, customer demand or cable age) cannot be controlled, so only observed historical data are available","When the team must prove cause and effect","When interactions between the X’s are the main interest"],answer:1,hint:"DOE needs X’s you can set; MLR works on passively collected data when the X’s cannot be set.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A DOE needs the team to **set** the X’s. When the inputs are uncontrollable (weather, demand, asset age), the best you can do is model the history with MLR."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Rainfall and cable age cannot be set at chosen levels, so MLR on historical records is the practical tool. Remember that it shows association, not proof of cause."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Controllable X’s, proof of cause and interest in interactions all point to a DOE."}],final:"Uncontrollable X’s → historical MLR → answer **B**",whyWrong:["If X’s can be set cheaply, a DOE is better: it gives clean, independent effects and proof of cause.","","Only a designed experiment proves cause and effect; MLR shows association.","Interactions are estimated directly in a DOE; basic MLR does not include them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-209",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does strong **multicollinearity** do to the coefficient table of a multiple regression?",options:["It inflates the standard errors of the correlated terms, so their P-Values look larger and their coefficients become unstable","It shrinks the standard errors, so the P-Values become too small","It always makes R-sq fall to zero","It makes the residuals non-normal"],answer:0,hint:"VIF = how much a coefficient’s variance is inflated by correlation with the other X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When X’s are correlated, the model struggles to give each one its own effect. The variance of their coefficients is inflated (hence the name **Variance** Inflation Factor)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Larger SE Coef → smaller |T| → larger P-Values. The coefficients can swing a lot, even change sign, when a row or term is added or removed. R-sq for the model as a whole can stay high."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Standard errors grow; they do not shrink. R-sq is not driven to zero. Residual normality is a separate assumption."}],final:"Inflated SEs and unstable coefficients → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The opposite: correlated X’s make the standard errors larger, not smaller.","Overall R-sq can stay high; it is the individual coefficients that become unreliable.","Multicollinearity is about the X’s, not about the distribution of the residuals."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-210",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book remove non-significant terms **one at a time** (largest P-Value first) and refit, rather than removing them all at once?",options:["Because Minitab can delete only one column per run","Because removing terms always lowers R-sq(adj)","Because the constant must always be removed first","Because when the X’s are related, the P-Values of the remaining terms change after each removal, so a term that looked weak may become significant"],answer:3,hint:"Each coefficient and P-Value depends on which other X’s are in the model.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Partial coefficients depend on the other terms in the model. Remove one X and the others’ coefficients, SEs and P-Values are recalculated."},{title:"Apply",body:"In the book’s flight-speed example, Temp was removed first. The model was refitted before Altitude (p = 0.067) and then Turbine angle (p = 0.093) were removed, one step at a time."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"This is a statistical reason, not a software limit. Removing a useless term usually **raises** R-sq(adj). The constant is not removed."}],final:"P-Values change after each removal → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Minitab can fit any set of terms; the reason is statistical, not technical.","Removing a weak term usually raises R-sq(adj), because the penalty falls.","The constant normally stays in the model; it is not part of the elimination.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-211",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In Best Subsets output, which **Mallows Cp** value points to a good candidate model?",options:["A large Cp, well above the number of terms","A Cp of exactly zero","A small Cp, close to or less than the number of terms in the model (X’s plus the constant)","Cp is not used; only R-sq matters"],answer:2,hint:"Mallows Cp: small and less than (or close to) the number of terms → little bias from missing X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Mallows Cp compares a subset model’s error with the full model’s. A large Cp means important X’s are missing (bias). The book says Cp should be **small and less than the number of terms**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Look for rows where Cp ≈ p (number of terms) or lower, together with high R-sq(adj) and low S."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A large Cp signals a biased model. Exactly zero is not the target. R-sq alone always favours the biggest model."}],final:"Small Cp ≤ number of terms → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A large Cp means the subset leaves out important X’s.","Zero is not the target; Cp near or below the number of terms is.","","The book lists Mallows Cp as one of the Best Subsets statistics to evaluate; R-sq alone always favours the largest model."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-212",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does **R-sq(pred)** in Minitab's Model Summary indicate?",options:["The % of variation explained after adjusting for the number of predictors","How well the model predicts observations that were not used to fit it (each point is left out in turn)","The confidence level used for the prediction interval","The correlation between the predicted and actual X values"],answer:1,hint:"R-sq(adj) = penalised fit; R-sq(pred) = leave-one-out predictive ability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq(pred) removes each observation in turn, refits, and predicts the left-out point. It measures how well the model works on **new** data."},{title:"Apply",body:"If R-sq(pred) is much lower than R-sq, the model is overfitted. It fits this sample well but will predict future data poorly."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The penalty for extra predictors is R-sq(adj). Confidence level is a separate choice. Predicted values are of Y, not X."}],final:"R-sq(pred) = predictive ability on new data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["That describes R-sq(adj), the penalised version of R-sq.","","The confidence level is chosen by the user and is not a model-fit statistic.","The model predicts Y, not X; R-sq(pred) concerns predictions of Y."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-213",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A model of **NPS score** on 3 predictors uses 2,000 survey responses. The overall regression P-Value is **0.000**, but **R-sq = 9%**. What is the correct reading?",options:["The model is useless, because a P-Value of 0.000 is impossible","The model explains 91% of the variation in NPS","A low R-sq proves that the predictors have no effect","The model is statistically significant but explains little of the variation, so it will predict individual NPS scores poorly"],answer:3,hint:"P-Value = is there any relationship? R-sq = how much of Y it explains. With a large n, tiny effects become significant.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Statistical significance and practical usefulness are different questions. With 2,000 responses, even weak relationships give p < 0.05."},{title:"Apply",body:"p = 0.000 says the X’s are related to NPS. R-sq = 9% says they explain very little, and 91% of the variation is left over. Look for other important X’s before using this model for decisions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"P = 0.000 just means below 0.0005. 91% is the **unexplained** share. A significant F shows that there is an effect."}],final:"Significant but weak → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Minitab rounds; 0.000 means p < 0.0005, which is entirely possible with large samples.","Read the complement: 9% is explained, 91% is not.","The significant F-test shows the predictors do have an effect, only a small one.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-214",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the Improve phase, what is the MAIN use of a verified multiple regression model?",options:["To prove cause and effect without further testing","To show which X’s matter most and at what settings to run them, before confirming the improvement with a pilot or DOE","To replace the control plan","To calculate the process sigma level"],answer:1,hint:"MLR → which X’s and what settings; DOE / pilot → proof; control plan → sustain.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A verified MLR points to the vital few X’s and how Y responds to them. Since it is based on historical data, the improvement is then confirmed by a pilot or a DOE."},{title:"Apply",body:"Use the coefficients and prediction intervals to choose target settings, test them, then lock them into the control plan."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"MLR does not prove cause. A control plan is a Control-phase document. Sigma level comes from defect data or capability, not from regression."}],final:"Choose key X’s and settings, then confirm → answer **B**",whyWrong:["MLR shows association only; cause and effect need a designed experiment or pilot.","","The control plan sustains the improvement; a regression model can feed it but not replace it.","Sigma level comes from defect rates or capability analysis, not from a regression model."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-215",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are steps in the book's multiple regression roadmap **EXCEPT**:",options:["Create a matrix plot of Y and the candidate X’s","Run Best Subsets and evaluate R-sq, R-sq(adj), Mallows Cp and S","Verify the final model with current process data","Box-Cox transform every X before any plot is drawn"],answer:3,hint:"Roadmap: matrix plot → Best Subsets → evaluate → iterate the fit → residuals → verify.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s MLR steps: 1 matrix plot, 2 Best Subsets, 3 evaluate R-sq, R-sq(adj), Cp, number of predictors and S, 4 fit iteratively, 5 analyse residuals, 6 verify with current data."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"Matrix plot, Best Subsets with its statistics, and verification are all on the list. A blanket Box-Cox of every X before looking at the data is not, because transformations are used only when a problem shows up."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:"The Box-Cox option."}],final:"Blanket Box-Cox is not a roadmap step → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Step 1 of the roadmap is a matrix plot.","Steps 2 and 3 are Best Subsets and evaluating its statistics.","Step 6 is verifying the model with current process data.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-216",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Each of the following can make a multiple regression coefficient misleading **EXCEPT**:",options:["Strong correlation between two of the X’s","Collecting a larger sample over the same range of X values","Large measurement error in that X","An important X, left out of the model, that moves together with it"],answer:1,hint:"Coefficients are distorted by collinearity, a noisy X and omitted correlated X’s; more data makes them more precise.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A partial coefficient is the effect of an X with the other X’s held constant. It goes wrong when X’s overlap (collinearity), when X is badly measured, or when a related X is missing and its effect is credited to the X that is in the model."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"Collinearity, measurement error and an omitted related X all distort the coefficient. A larger sample reduces the standard errors and makes the coefficients **more** trustworthy."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:"The larger sample option."}],final:"More data does not mislead → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Collinear X’s give unstable, even wrong-signed, coefficients.","","Measurement error in an X weakens and blurs its coefficient.","An omitted X that moves with an included X is credited to it, which biases the coefficient."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-217",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multiple regression, the predictor with the **largest coefficient** is always the one with the strongest effect on Y. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Coefficient size depends on the units of X; compare T-Values (or standardized effects) instead.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A coefficient is "change in Y per **one unit** of X". Change the units (km to m, hours to minutes) and the coefficient changes size, while the effect stays the same.'},{title:"Apply",body:"To judge the strength of evidence, compare |T-Values| and P-Values. To judge practical impact, multiply each coefficient by a realistic change in its X. So the statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Units drive coefficient size: 0.041 per km is 41 per 1,000 km, and the effect is the same.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-218",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for dealing with **multicollinearity** in the correct order:

- **P** — Refit the model without the dropped X
- **Q** — Spot VIFs above 5 for two related X’s in the coefficient table
- **R** — Re-check the VIFs and P-Values of the remaining terms
- **S** — Use process knowledge to decide which of the two related X’s to drop`,options:["Q → P → S → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → S → R → P","Q → S → P → R"],answer:3,hint:"Detect → decide → refit → re-check.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Find the collinear pair first, then decide which X to keep (the one that is easier to control or more meaningful), refit, and confirm that the problem is gone."},{title:"Order the steps",body:"**Q** spot high VIFs → **S** decide which X to drop → **P** refit → **R** re-check VIFs and P-Values. In the book’s flight example, Temp (VIF 5.4) was dropped and the refitted VIFs were 1.1–1.9."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["You cannot refit without first deciding which X to drop.","The decision comes after the high VIFs have been spotted.","VIFs and P-Values can only be re-checked after the model has been refitted.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-219",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`A Black Belt has a final MLR model for **provisioning time**. Put these steps in the correct order:

- **P** — Confirm the improvement with a pilot or designed experiment
- **Q** — Verify the model against current process data
- **R** — Use the model to choose target settings for the key X’s
- **S** — Add the key X settings and their checks to the control plan`,options:["Q → R → P → S","R → Q → P → S","Q → P → R → S","Q → R → S → P"],answer:0,hint:"Verify → use → confirm → sustain.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A model is used only after it has been verified with current data. Settings chosen from historical data are then confirmed in practice before they are standardised."},{title:"Order the steps",body:"**Q** verify → **R** choose settings → **P** confirm by pilot or DOE → **S** control plan."}],final:"Q → R → P → S → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The model must be verified before it is used to choose settings.","You cannot pilot settings that have not been chosen yet.","Settings go into the control plan only after a pilot or DOE has confirmed them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-220",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A report shows Minitab’s 95% prediction interval for **site-visit duration** at the planned settings as **(31.2, 46.8) min**, but the Fit column was cut off. What is the Fit?",options:["7.8 min","39.0 min","15.6 min","78.0 min"],answer:1,hint:"CI and PI are symmetric around the Fit: $\\hat{Y}=\\dfrac{\\text{lower}+\\text{upper}}{2}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Lower PI limit = 31.2 min, upper PI limit = 46.8 min."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"Both intervals are Fit ± a margin, so the Fit is the midpoint: $$\\hat{Y}=\\dfrac{L+U}{2}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=\\dfrac{31.2+46.8}{2}=\\dfrac{78.0}{2}=39.0$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The model predicts 39.0 min, and a single new visit is expected (95%) to take between 31.2 and 46.8 min. The margin is ±7.8 min."}],final:"Fit = 39.0 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is the half-width, (46.8 − 31.2) ÷ 2: the margin, not the centre.","","This is the full width of the interval, 46.8 − 31.2.","Added the two limits but forgot to divide by 2."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-221",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A model of **average queue wait (s)** on **agents on shift** was fitted on data from 10 to 34 agents (mean 21.5 agents):

\`\`\`
Prediction for Wait_s

Agents    Fit   SE Fit       95% CI          95% PI
  22     58.4    0.92   (56.5, 60.3)    (47.9, 68.9)
  33     31.7    2.10   (27.4, 36.0)    (20.5, 42.9)
\`\`\`

Why is the CI at **33 agents** more than twice as wide as the CI at 22 agents?`,options:["Because S, the model’s scatter, is larger at 33 agents","Because the Fit at 33 agents is smaller","Because 33 agents is far from the mean of the X data (21.5), where the fitted line is less certain; 22 agents is close to the centre","Because the PI is wider than the CI"],answer:2,hint:"Interval width grows with the distance of X₀ from X̄; both bands are narrowest at the mean of X.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CI half-width contains $\\dfrac{(X_0-\\bar{X})^2}{S_{xx}}$, so the further X₀ is from $\\bar{X}$, the larger SE Fit and the wider the CI."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"X₀ = 22 is only 0.5 from the mean, so SE Fit = 0.92. X₀ = 33 is 11.5 from the mean, near the edge of the data, so SE Fit = 2.10. A larger SE Fit gives a wider CI. Both PIs are about ±10.5 to ±11.2, because S dominates them."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"S is one value for the whole model. The size of the Fit does not change the width. The PI being wider is true but does not explain the difference between the two CIs."}],final:"Distance from X̄ → wider CI → answer **C**",whyWrong:["S is a single value for the whole model; it is the same at 22 and at 33 agents.","The level of the Fit does not affect the interval width; SE Fit does.","","The PI is always wider than the CI, but that does not explain why the two CIs differ."],trap:"Both bands are narrowest at X̄ and flare towards the ends of the data.",bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-222",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`The same **SIM-activation time** model was used twice at the same setting, once with a 90% and once with a 95% confidence level:

\`\`\`
Run   Fit        CI              PI
 1    43.2   (41.6, 44.8)   (36.0, 50.4)
 2    43.2   (41.9, 44.5)   (37.2, 49.2)
\`\`\`

Which statement is correct?`,options:["Run 1 used 95%, because a higher confidence level makes both intervals wider","Run 2 used 95%, because a narrower interval is more confident","Run 1 has a larger S than Run 2","The confidence level cannot be identified from intervals"],answer:0,hint:"Higher confidence level → larger t-value → wider CI and wider PI.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"To be more confident of catching the true value, the interval must be wider. Going from 90% to 95% raises t, which widens both the CI and the PI."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Run 1: CI ±1.6, PI ±7.2. Run 2: CI ±1.3, PI ±6.0. Both of Run 1’s intervals are wider by the same factor, about 1.2 (close to 2.09 ÷ 1.72 for t), so Run 1 is the 95% run."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Narrow means more precise, not more confident. S is the same because it is the same model. Width does reveal the level when everything else is equal."}],final:"Run 1 = 95% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Mixed up precision with confidence: the narrower intervals belong to the lower (90%) level.","Both runs use the same model, so S is identical; only the t-value changed.","With the same model and setting, the wider pair must come from the higher confidence level."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-223",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Prediction from a two-X model of **fibre-install time (h)** (S = 0.82):

\`\`\`
Prediction for Install_h
Install_h = 1.85 + 0.0640 Distance_km + 0.420 Splices

Settings: Distance_km = 25, Splices = 6

   Fit   SE Fit       95% CI            95% PI
 5.970    0.180   (5.601, 6.339)   (4.247, 7.693)
\`\`\`

What does **SE Fit = 0.180** measure?`,options:["S, the typical scatter of individual jobs around the model","The standard error of the Distance_km coefficient","The half-width of the 95% PI","The standard error of the estimated mean install time at these settings, used to build the CI"],answer:3,hint:"SE Fit = uncertainty of the fitted mean at X₀ (CI); PI also adds S for individual scatter.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"SE Fit is the standard error of Ŷ, the estimated **mean** response at the chosen settings. It reflects how well the line itself is known."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"CI = Fit ± t × SE Fit. The PI uses $\\sqrt{S^2+SE_{Fit}^2}=\\sqrt{0.82^2+0.18^2}=0.839$, which is why it is much wider than the CI."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"S = 0.82 is the scatter of single jobs. Coefficient SEs are in the coefficient table. The PI half-width here is about 1.72, not 0.18."}],final:"SE Fit = SE of the fitted mean → answer **D**",whyWrong:["S = 0.82 is the scatter of individual jobs; SE Fit (0.180) is much smaller.","Coefficient standard errors appear in the Coefficients table, not in the prediction output.","The PI half-width is (7.693 − 4.247) ÷ 2 ≈ 1.72, not 0.18.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-224",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt studies how interval width at $\\bar{X}$ changes with sample size for a model with **S = 4.0 min**:

| n (data points) | 95% CI half-width | 95% PI half-width |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 10 | 2.92 | 9.67 |
| 30 | 1.50 | 8.33 |
| 120 | 0.72 | 7.95 |

What does the table show?`,options:["Both intervals shrink towards zero at the same rate as n grows","The CI keeps shrinking towards zero as n grows, but the PI levels off near ±t·S (about ±8 min), because single values still scatter around the line","The PI shrinks faster than the CI","Neither interval depends on n"],answer:1,hint:"CI half-width $\\propto S\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n}}$; PI half-width $\\propto S\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{1}{n}}$, so the PI can never shrink below about t·S.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'At $\\bar{X}$: CI = $t\\,S\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n}}$ and PI = $t\\,S\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{1}{n}}$. The "1" in the PI is the scatter of a single point, and more data cannot remove it.'},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"CI: 2.92 → 1.50 → 0.72, which keeps falling. PI: 9.67 → 8.33 → 7.95, which flattens out near t × S = 1.98 × 4.0 ≈ 7.9."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The two intervals do not shrink at the same rate. The PI shrinks more slowly, not faster. Both clearly change with n."}],final:"CI → 0, PI → about ±t·S → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The PI falls only from 9.67 to 7.95, while the CI falls by a factor of 4.","","The CI more than halves at each step; the PI changes only a little.","Both columns change with n; the CI changes a lot."],trap:"More data narrows the CI a lot, but the PI never falls below the natural scatter.",bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-225",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A regression of **enterprise-circuit delivery days** on **route distance** has R-sq = 31% and S = 9.5 days. At 40 km the 95% PI is **(6, 45) days**. Sales wants to quote each customer a delivery date from this model. What is the BEST advice?",options:["Quote the Fit, because it is the best single estimate","Quote the 95% CI instead, because it is narrower","The PI is too wide to be useful for single orders; look for more X’s (multiple regression) to reduce S before quoting dates","Quote the lower PI limit so that customers are pleasantly surprised"],answer:2,hint:"PI width is driven mostly by S; a weak model (low R-sq, large S) gives PIs too wide for promises to single customers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a single order the honest range is the PI. Its width depends mostly on S. With R-sq = 31%, most of the variation is unexplained, so S is large."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 6–45 day range gives no basis for a commitment. Find the missing drivers (permits, civil works, crew availability) and model them, so that S and the PI shrink."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Fit alone hides the uncertainty. The CI is about the average order, not this customer’s order. The lower limit would be missed by about 97.5% of orders."}],final:"Improve the model before quoting → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Fit hides the ±19-day uncertainty; many orders would miss a date quoted from it.","The CI describes the average order, not a single customer’s order; quoting it would understate the risk.","","Only about 2.5% of orders would finish by the lower PI limit; most customers would be let down."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-226",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`For **call wait time**, a model gives:

- at **12 agents**: 95% CI (40.1, 44.0) s, 95% PI (30.2, 53.9) s
- at **14 agents**: 95% CI (35.2, 38.9) s, 95% PI (25.3, 48.8) s

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["There is no difference between 12 and 14 agents, because the PIs overlap","Every call will wait less with 14 agents than with 12","Because the CIs do not overlap, the PIs cannot overlap either","The mean wait is lower with 14 agents (the CIs do not overlap), but single calls at the two staffing levels still overlap a lot, so some calls at 14 agents will wait longer than some at 12"],answer:3,hint:"CI → compare averages; PI → range of single values. Separate CIs can sit inside heavily overlapping PIs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"CIs describe where the **mean** is; PIs describe where **individual** calls fall. The PI is always much wider."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The CIs (40.1–44.0 and 35.2–38.9) do not overlap, so the mean wait really is about 5 s lower with 14 agents. The PIs overlap from 30.2 to 48.8 s, so many single calls at 14 agents still wait longer than many calls at 12."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Overlapping PIs do not hide a difference in means. "Every call" ignores individual scatter. Separate CIs say nothing about PIs.'}],final:"Mean improves, individuals overlap → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Overlapping PIs are normal even when the means clearly differ; compare means with the CIs.","Individual calls scatter widely, so a lower mean does not make every call shorter.","PIs are much wider than CIs, so they can overlap even when the CIs are well apart.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-227",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A fitted line plot of **wait time per call (s)** against **agents on shift** shows the fitted line with 95% CI and PI bands; wait falls as agents rise. The service promise is that a **single call** waits **no more than 60 s**, with 95% confidence. Where should the minimum staffing be read from the plot?",options:["Where the fitted line crosses 60 s","Where the upper 95% CI band crosses 60 s","Where the upper 95% PI band crosses 60 s","Where the lower 95% PI band crosses 60 s"],answer:2,hint:"Promise about single values → PI; promise of a maximum → use the upper band.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'A promise for **each** call concerns individual values, so use the PI. For a maximum ("no more than 60 s"), the **upper** band must sit at or below the limit.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Follow the 60 s line until it meets the upper PI band, then drop down to the X axis. Staffing at or above that point keeps single calls under 60 s with 95% confidence. This mirrors the book’s mailing example, which used the lower PI band to guarantee a minimum response."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"At the fitted line, about half of all calls would wait longer than 60 s. The CI band is about the mean. The lower PI band is the one used for a minimum guarantee."}],final:"Upper PI band at 60 s → answer **C**",whyWrong:["At the fitted line about half of all single calls would still exceed 60 s.","The CI band covers the average wait, not single calls, so too many calls would exceed 60 s.","","The lower PI band is for guaranteeing a minimum; here the promise is a maximum."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-228",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A model of **repair time** on **cable age** was fitted with 80 jobs: 75 jobs had cable aged 1–12 years and only 5 had cable aged 18–25 years. The 95% CI at **22 years** is about **four times** as wide as at 7 years. What is the BEST explanation and action?",options:["S is larger at 22 years, so Y should be transformed","Few data points support the line at 22 years, far from the mean age, so the estimate there is uncertain; collect more data on old cable before relying on it","The model is invalid at all ages","Use the PI instead, because it is narrower"],answer:1,hint:"CI width depends on (X₀ − X̄)² and on how much data supports that region of X.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CI widens where X₀ is far from X̄ and where few points pin down the line. The book notes the CI flares at the extremes because less data support it there."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Five old-cable jobs give little support at 22 years, which is far from the mean age of about 7–8 years. The prediction there is legitimate but imprecise. Gather more old-cable jobs if decisions depend on that region."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"S is one value for the model. A wide CI at one end does not make the whole model invalid. The PI is always wider than the CI."}],final:"Little data far from X̄ → wide CI → answer **B**",whyWrong:["S is one value for the whole model; it is the width term (X₀ − X̄)² that grows.","","The model can be precise near the centre and imprecise at the edge; it is not invalid everywhere.","The PI is always wider than the CI, never narrower."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-229",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"For a model of **dropped calls per cell per day**, the 95% PI at low traffic is **(−3.1, 8.4)**. What does the negative lower limit MOST likely show?",options:["Some cells will have a negative number of dropped calls","The confidence level is too low","The CI should be used instead, because it can never go below zero","The normal, constant-variance error assumption does not suit small counts near zero; consider a transformation (for example √Y) or report the lower limit as 0"],answer:3,hint:"Intervals assume normal residuals with equal spread; bounded data near a limit (counts near 0) break this.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Regression intervals are Fit ± t × (standard error), which assumes normal, equal-variance errors. Counts near zero are skewed and bounded, so a symmetric interval can cross the impossible zero line."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A negative count is impossible, so the interval is a sign the model form does not fit this region. A √ or log transformation of Y (Box-Cox) often fixes this. As a minimum, the lower limit is reported as 0."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The data cannot be negative. A higher confidence level widens the interval and makes it worse. A CI can also cross zero and answers a different question."}],final:"Model assumptions do not suit counts near zero → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Dropped calls cannot be negative; the interval, not the process, is at fault.","Raising the confidence level widens the interval and pushes the lower limit further below zero.","A CI is about the mean, answers a different question, and can also cross zero.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–462"},{id:"sv-4-230",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Minitab shows a 95% CI of **(0.10, 0.18)** for the **coefficient of Backlog** in a restore-time model. What does this interval describe?",options:["The range for the mean restore time at the average backlog","The range for a single new incident’s restore time","The range likely to contain the true slope: the change in mean restore time per extra backlog ticket, with the other X’s held constant","The range of backlog values used to fit the model"],answer:2,hint:"A CI for a coefficient is about the slope; a CI for the mean response is about Ŷ at given X’s; a PI is about one new Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Three different intervals appear in regression: for a **coefficient** (slope), for the **mean response** at chosen settings (CI) and for a **single new value** (PI)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"This one belongs to a coefficient, so it covers the true change in mean restore time per extra ticket (0.10–0.18 h). It does not contain 0, which agrees with Backlog being significant."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The mean response and a single new value are given in the Prediction output, not next to the coefficient. The data range of X is not an interval estimate."}],final:"Interval for the true slope → answer **C**",whyWrong:["That would be the CI for the mean response, shown in the Prediction output.","That would be a prediction interval, shown in the Prediction output.","","The data range of X is a description of the sample, not a confidence interval."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458, 462–469"},{id:"sv-4-231",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does the **95%** in a 95% **prediction interval** mean?",options:["The model explains 95% of the variation in Y","95% of the data used to fit the model fall inside the CI band","The mean response is within 5% of the Fit","If the method were repeated many times, about 95% of such intervals would contain the next individual observation at that X"],answer:3,hint:"Confidence level = long-run hit rate of the interval method, not R-sq and not a percentage error.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The confidence level describes the **method**: intervals built this way capture their target 95% of the time. For a PI, the target is a single new observation at that X."},{title:"Apply",body:"So about 1 in 20 new observations at that X would fall outside a 95% PI. This is why the PI is used for single-value promises."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"R-sq is a separate statistic. Most data points fall outside the narrow CI band. 95% is a coverage rate, not a percentage error around the Fit."}],final:"95% = coverage of the next single observation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["That is R-sq, which is unrelated to the confidence level.","The CI band is narrow and most individual points fall outside it; it targets the mean.","The confidence level is a coverage rate, not a ±5% error band.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-232",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In a multiple regression with three X’s, a CI or PI is calculated for…",options:["each X separately, ignoring the other X’s","a specific combination of settings of all the X’s in the model","only the X with the smallest P-Value","the overall mean of Y in the data set"],answer:1,hint:"Ŷ in MLR depends on all X’s, so its CI and PI are for one full set of X settings.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The fitted value Ŷ = b₀ + b₁X₁ + b₂X₂ + b₃X₃ needs a value for **every** X. The CI and PI belong to that combination."},{title:"Apply",body:"Minitab’s Predict asks for a setting for each X and returns the Fit, SE Fit, CI and PI for that point. The intervals widen as the combination moves away from the centre of the X data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Setting one X alone gives no prediction. The P-Values do not choose which X is used. The overall mean of Y ignores the X’s."}],final:"One full set of X settings → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A prediction needs every X in the model; one X alone does not define Ŷ.","","All X’s in the model are used, whatever their P-Values.","The overall mean of Y ignores the X’s and is not a regression prediction."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-233",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following make a 95% **prediction interval** NARROWER **EXCEPT**:",options:["A model with a smaller S","Predicting at an X closer to the mean of the X data","Raising the confidence level to 99%","Fitting the model with more observations"],answer:2,hint:"PI half-width = $t\\,S\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{1}{n}+\\dfrac{(X_0-\\bar{X})^2}{S_{xx}}}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The PI shrinks when S falls, when n rises and when X₀ moves towards X̄. It widens when the confidence level (and so t) rises."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"A smaller S, X₀ closer to X̄ and more data all narrow the PI. 99% instead of 95% raises t (about 2.0 → 2.7), which widens it."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:"The 99% option."}],final:"99% confidence widens the PI → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A smaller S directly shrinks the PI, which is dominated by S.","Moving X₀ towards X̄ makes the (X₀ − X̄)² term smaller.","","A larger n shrinks the 1 ÷ n term and lowers t a little, so the PI narrows somewhat."],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-234",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"With enough data, the width of a 95% **prediction interval** can be made as close to zero as you like. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"The PI includes the scatter of single values (S), which more data cannot remove.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'PI half-width = $t\\,S\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{1}{n}+\\cdots}$. As n grows, only the $\\dfrac{1}{n}$ part disappears; the "1" stays, so the PI tends to ±t·S.'},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the **CI** can be squeezed towards zero with more data. Single values still scatter by about S, so the statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["That is true only for the CI; the PI keeps the individual scatter S.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 454–458"},{id:"sv-4-235",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`After fitting a three-predictor model of **complaint-handling time**, the Black Belt tests the residuals for normality (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Probability Plot of RESI1
Normal

Mean      3.1E-15
StDev     1.412
N         45
AD        1.384
P-Value  <0.005
\`\`\`

The histogram of the residuals has a long right tail. What is the correct conclusion and next step?`,options:["Fail to reject normality: the residuals are fine because their mean is essentially zero","Accept H₀ that the residuals are normal, because the AD statistic is above 1","Reject normality of the residuals: look for unusual points and consider transforming Y (for example with Box-Cox), then refit and re-check","The model is biased, because the residual mean is not exactly zero"],answer:2,hint:"Residual normality test: p < α → reject normality → investigate outliers / transform Y and refit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Normal residuals are one of the regression assumptions. The P-Values and the CIs and PIs rely on it. A normality test with p < α rejects normality."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"p < 0.005 < 0.05, so reject H₀: the residuals are not normal. A long right tail is typical of time data. Check the largest residuals for special causes, then try transforming Y (often ln or √, chosen by Box-Cox), refit and re-test."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Least-squares residuals always average zero (3.1E-15 is zero), so the mean tells you nothing about normality. "Accept H₀" is wrong wording, and a large AD points towards rejecting, not accepting.'}],final:"Reject normality → investigate and transform → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The residual mean is always zero with a constant in the model; the P-Value below 0.005 rejects normality.",'Never "accept" H₀; a large AD with p < 0.005 means reject normality.',"","3.1E-15 is zero to rounding; least-squares residuals always sum to zero, so there is no bias here."],trap:"Residual mean = 0 is automatic; it is not evidence of normality.",bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470, 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-236",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A model of **daily outdoor-cabinet alarms** uses traffic and equipment age (n = 40). The Black Belt saves the residuals and regresses them on **cabinet temperature**, which is **not** in the model:

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: RESI1 versus Temp_C

Term       Coef   SE Coef   T-Value   P-Value
Constant  -2.84      0.88     -3.23     0.003
Temp_C    0.112     0.034      3.29     0.002

R-sq = 22.1%
\`\`\`

What does this suggest?`,options:["The model is fine, because residuals always average zero","Temperature explains part of what the current model misses; consider adding Temp_C as a predictor, then refit and re-check","The residuals are not normally distributed","The constant should be removed from the original model"],answer:1,hint:"A residual pattern against a variable not in the model means that variable carries missing information.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Residuals are the part of Y the model has not explained. If they are related to another variable, that variable holds information the model is missing."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Temp_C is significant (p = 0.002) and explains 22% of the leftover variation. Add temperature to the model (check its VIF) and re-run the residual analysis."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A zero mean is automatic and does not rule out a pattern. This output says nothing about the shape of the distribution. The constant here belongs to the auxiliary regression, not to the main model."}],final:"Missing X (temperature) → add and refit → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A zero residual mean is automatic; a significant link to Temp_C shows the residuals are not random.","","Normality is checked with a normal plot or test; this output tests a relationship with temperature.","The constant here belongs to the residual regression and says nothing about the main model’s constant."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-237",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"On the normal probability plot of residuals from a **ticket-age model**, the points follow the line in the middle but **bend away at both ends**: the lowest residuals are too low and the highest are too high (heavy tails). What does this indicate?",options:["Non-constant variance","Residuals that depend on the time order","A missing quadratic term","Residuals that are not normally distributed"],answer:3,hint:"Normal plot → normality; vs fits → equal variance and curvature; vs order → independence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each residual plot checks one assumption. The **normal probability plot** checks normality. Points bending away from the line at the ends mean the tails do not match a normal distribution."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Too-extreme points at both ends are heavy tails, so normality is in doubt. Check the extreme points, and confirm with a normality test before deciding to transform."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Unequal variance and a missing curve are seen in residuals versus fits. Dependence is seen in residuals versus order."}],final:"Non-normal residuals → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Non-constant variance shows up as a funnel in residuals versus fits, not on the normal plot.","Dependence over time is read from the residuals-versus-order plot.","A missing curve shows as a U-shape in residuals versus fits or versus an X.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-238",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A model of **daily care-call volume** uses 120 consecutive days. The **residuals-versus-order** plot shows a repeating saw-tooth: high residuals every Sunday and Monday and low ones mid-week. What does this indicate, and what should be done?",options:["The residuals are not independent: a day-of-week effect is missing, so add it (for example as indicator variables)","The residuals are non-normal, so transform Y","The variance is not constant, so use weighted regression","Nothing: any pattern in time order is expected"],answer:0,hint:"A pattern (trend, cycle, runs) in residuals versus order means the independence assumption fails; look for a missing time-related X.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Residuals versus order checks **independence**. A repeating cycle means consecutive residuals are linked to the day, so information is missing."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A weekly saw-tooth points to a day-of-week effect. Add day type (for example weekend/Monday versus mid-week indicators), refit and re-check the order plot."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A cycle in time order is not a normality problem or a funnel. A healthy order plot shows no pattern at all."}],final:"Dependence → add the missing day-of-week X → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Normality is read from the normal plot; the problem here is a time pattern.","Non-constant variance shows as a funnel in residuals versus fits, not as a weekly cycle.","A healthy residuals-versus-order plot shows random scatter; a cycle is a warning."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-239",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In a **site-installation cost** model, Obs 31 has St Resid = 3.4 (flag R). The record is correct: it was a rooftop site that needed a crane, which happens on about 1 in 15 installations. A colleague wants to delete it to raise R-sq from 81% to 86%. What should the Black Belt do?",options:["Delete it, because any point with |St Resid| > 3 must be removed","Delete it together with the two nearest points, to keep the data balanced",'Keep it: it is a real, recurring part of the process; consider adding a "crane needed" X rather than deleting data to improve R-sq',"Replace its cost with the model’s Fit"],answer:2,hint:"Investigate flagged points; remove only proven errors; never delete valid data just to raise R-sq.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An R flag means the model fits the point badly. It is not automatically wrong data. The book’s advice: be careful, investigate, and never remove points just to improve the fit."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'The record is valid and the situation recurs (about 7% of sites). Removing it would hide a real cost driver. A "crane needed" indicator would explain it properly.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"There is no automatic deletion rule at |3|. Deleting extra points or replacing values with Fits is data manipulation."}],final:"Keep it and model the cause → answer **C**",whyWrong:["There is no automatic deletion rule; a valid, recurring observation must stay.","Deleting more points makes the problem worse and has no justification.","","Replacing real data with the model’s own prediction is data manipulation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-240",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which residual plot is used to check the assumption of **equal (constant) variance** in a multiple regression?",options:["Normal probability plot of the residuals","Residuals versus order","Residuals versus fitted values","Histogram of the residuals"],answer:2,hint:"Normal plot / histogram → normality; vs fits → equal variance; vs order → independence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The four-in-one residual plots each test one assumption, and the pairing is the same in multiple regression as in simple regression."},{title:"Apply",body:"Equal variance means the spread of the residuals is the same at all fitted values, so plot residuals against the **fits**. A funnel shape means unequal variance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The normal plot and the histogram check normality. Residuals versus order checks independence."}],final:"Residuals vs fits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The normal probability plot checks normality, not equal spread.","Residuals versus order checks independence over time.","","The histogram shows the shape of the distribution (normality), not how spread changes with the fitted value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-241",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A model has R-sq = 95% and all P-Values below 0.01. Why must the residuals still be analysed?",options:["Because a high R-sq is only possible when the residuals are normal","Because the residuals are needed to calculate R-sq(adj)","They need not be: a high R-sq proves that the assumptions are met","Because the P-Values, confidence intervals and prediction intervals are only valid if the residual assumptions hold"],answer:3,hint:"Fit statistics do not check assumptions; residual plots do.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'R-sq and P-Values come from the model’s calculations. They can be trusted only if the residuals are normal, independent and of equal variance. The book calls the residual check "VITAL".'},{title:"Apply",body:"A 95% R-sq model can still have a funnel, a time trend or a missing curve. Any of these makes its P-Values and intervals misleading."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"High R-sq can coexist with non-normal residuals. R-sq(adj) comes from the sums of squares. A good fit does not prove the assumptions."}],final:"Inference depends on residual assumptions → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A high R-sq can occur with skewed or patterned residuals.","R-sq(adj) comes from the sums of squares and n and p; no residual plot is needed for it.","A good fit says nothing about whether the residuals are normal, independent and equal in spread.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-242",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why can an observation with **high leverage** (flag **X**) matter even though its **residual is small**?",options:["Because its unusual X values let it pull the fitted model towards itself; its residual is small partly because it is shaping the fit","Because a small residual always signals a data-entry error","Because leverage measures how badly the point fits in the Y direction","It does not matter: only R-flagged points need attention"],answer:0,hint:"Leverage = how unusual the X values are (influence on the fit); R flag = large standardized residual (poor fit in Y).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A point far from the other X values acts like a lever. The fitted model bends towards it, so its own residual can look small even when it is steering the coefficients."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book’s flight example shows Obs 1 with St Resid −0.38 but flag X. Check whether the model changes much with and without such a point (influence check d) and whether its X values are genuine."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A small residual is not a sign of error. Poor fit in the Y direction is the R flag. X-flagged points can control the model, so they deserve a look too."}],final:"Leverage pulls the fit → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A small residual says nothing about data errors; the issue is the point’s influence.","That describes the R flag (a large standardized residual); leverage is about unusual X values.","The book asks you to confirm that no single point overly influences the model, which includes X-flagged points."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-243",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following residual patterns point to a problem with a multiple regression model **EXCEPT**:",options:["A funnel that widens as the fitted values increase","A run of 12 positive residuals followed by 12 negative ones in time order","A U-shaped band in residuals versus one of the X’s","About 2 of 45 standardized residuals just beyond ±2, with no pattern"],answer:3,hint:"Funnel = unequal variance; runs in order = dependence; U-shape = missing curvature; about 5% beyond ±2 = normal behaviour.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With normal residuals, about 5% of standardized residuals fall beyond ±2 by chance. Patterns are what matter."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"A funnel breaks equal variance. Long runs in time order break independence. A U-shape against an X means a squared term is missing. 2 of 45 (4.4%) just beyond ±2, with no pattern, is what chance alone produces."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:'The "2 of 45" option.'}],final:"About 5% beyond ±2 is expected → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A widening funnel means non-constant variance.","Long runs in time order mean the residuals are not independent.","A U-shape against an X means that X needs a curvature term.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-244",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multiple regression, if all four residual plots look healthy, **multicollinearity** among the X’s cannot be a problem. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Residual plots check the error assumptions; VIF checks whether the X’s are correlated with each other.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multicollinearity is about the X’s being correlated with **each other**. It does not distort the residuals. It inflates the coefficients’ standard errors."},{title:"Apply",body:"A model can have perfect-looking residuals and still have VIFs of 15. Both checks are needed, so the statement is false."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Residual plots cannot detect correlated X’s; only VIF (or a matrix plot of the X’s) can.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-245",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put these steps for handling an **R-flagged observation** in the correct order:

- **P** — Refit the final model and re-check all the residual plots
- **Q** — Note the flagged observation in Minitab’s Unusual Observations list
- **R** — Check the source record and the process conditions for that observation
- **S** — Correct it, keep it, or (with a documented reason) exclude it`,options:["Q → S → R → P","Q → R → S → P","R → Q → S → P","Q → R → P → S"],answer:1,hint:"Spot → investigate → decide → refit and re-check.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An unusual point is first found, then investigated. Only then is it corrected, kept or excluded, and the model is checked again."},{title:"Order the steps",body:"**Q** note the flag → **R** check the record and conditions → **S** decide and document → **P** refit and re-check the residuals."}],final:"Q → R → S → P → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Deciding before investigating risks deleting valid data.","","You cannot investigate a point before it has been identified.","The final refit comes after the decision about the point, not before it."],bookRef:"Book pp. 466–470"},{id:"sv-4-246",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"Handle times were transformed with **λ = −1**, so Y′ = 1 ÷ Y (Y in minutes). The mean on the transformed scale is **0.25**. What does this centre value back-transform to in original minutes?",options:["16.00 min","0.25 min","4.00 min","0.0625 min"],answer:2,hint:"Undo the transform with its inverse: for $Y^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1}{Y}$, the inverse is $Y=\\dfrac{1}{Y^{\\prime}}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"λ = −1 (reciprocal), transformed centre Y′ = 0.25."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Y=\\dfrac{1}{Y^{\\prime}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Y=\\dfrac{1}{0.25}=4.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The centre is about 4.00 min. With a reciprocal transform, larger Y′ means **shorter** times, so watch the direction when you report results."}],final:"Back-transformed centre = 4.00 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Back-transformed as if λ = −0.5 (1 ÷ 0.25², the reciprocal square root) instead of λ = −1.","Forgot to back-transform: 0.25 is on the reciprocal scale, not in minutes.","","Squared the transformed value (0.25²) instead of taking its reciprocal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-247",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Box-Cox recommends **λ = 0.5** for **delivery lead times (h)**. The specification is **LSL = 9 h** and **USL = 49 h**. What limits must be used on the transformed scale?",options:["LSL′ = 4.5, USL′ = 24.5","LSL′ = 81, USL′ = 2401","LSL′ = 9, USL′ = 49","LSL′ = 3, USL′ = 7"],answer:3,hint:"λ = 0.5 means $Y^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{Y}$; every external reference point (spec, target) gets the same transform.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"λ = 0.5 (square root), LSL = 9 h, USL = 49 h."},{title:"Pick the rule",body:"Book rule 4: all external reference points must use the same transform as the data."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$LSL^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\qquad USL^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{49}=7$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Run the capability analysis on √(lead time) against limits 3 and 7. With a positive λ the order of the limits stays the same."}],final:"Transformed specs 3 and 7 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Halved the limits instead of taking their square roots.","Squared the limits (λ = 2) instead of taking square roots (λ = 0.5).","Left the specs on the original scale, which breaks the rule that reference points get the same transform.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-248",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab’s Box-Cox output for 50 **cable-fault repair times (h)**:

\`\`\`
Box-Cox Transformation: Repair_h

                Estimate     95% CI
Lambda             -0.47   (-0.88, -0.05)
Rounded Value      -0.50
\`\`\`

Which transformation should be used?`,options:["Y′ = 1 ÷ √Y (reciprocal square root)","Y′ = √Y","Y′ = ln Y","No transformation, because the CI includes 1"],answer:0,hint:"λ table: 1 = none, 0.5 = √Y, 0 = ln Y, −0.5 = 1 ÷ √Y, −1 = 1 ÷ Y. Use the rounded value inside the CI.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab estimates λ, then suggests a convenient rounded value inside the 95% CI. The power Y^λ with λ = −0.5 is the reciprocal square root."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Rounded λ = −0.50, inside (−0.88, −0.05). The CI excludes 0 (log) and 1 (no transform), so use Y′ = 1 ÷ √Y. Then re-check normality on the transformed data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"√Y needs λ = 0.5, which is positive. ln Y needs λ = 0, which is outside the CI. The CI clearly does not include 1."}],final:"λ = −0.5 → 1 ÷ √Y → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The square root is λ = +0.5; this output points to a negative λ.","λ = 0 (log) lies outside the CI of −0.88 to −0.05.","The CI runs from −0.88 to −0.05, so it does not include 1."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-249",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt needs a capability estimate for **order-completion time** (all values positive). Output (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Normality Test (Anderson-Darling)
                    AD    P-Value
Order_h          3.912     <0.005
ln(Order_h)      0.861      0.024

Box-Cox: rounded lambda = 0, 95% CI for lambda (-0.31, 0.22)
\`\`\`

What should she do next?`,options:["Report capability on ln(Order_h), because the AD statistic dropped a lot","Accept normality, because the P-Value rose from below 0.005 to 0.024","The log transform did not make the data normal (p = 0.024 < 0.05): check for mixed populations or special causes and, if there are none, find a better-fitting distribution (Individual Distribution Identification) before running capability","Use λ = 1, since the transform did not work"],answer:2,hint:"Always re-test normality after transforming; if it still fails, look for mixed populations or use a non-normal distribution fit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A transform is only useful if the transformed data pass the normality check. The book confirms the result with a normality test (p = 0.687 after √ in its example)."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"p = 0.024 < 0.05, so reject normality even after the log. First check for mixed populations (two order types, two teams) or special causes. If the data are clean, use Individual Distribution Identification (for example Weibull) and run a non-normal capability analysis."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'An improved AD is not a pass. Never "accept" normality, and 0.024 is still below α. λ = 1 means no transform and the raw data fail badly.'}],final:"Still non-normal → investigate, then fit another distribution → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A smaller AD is better but p = 0.024 still rejects normality; capability on this scale would be unreliable.",'0.024 is still below 0.05, so normality is rejected; and H₀ is never "accepted".',"","λ = 1 means no transform; the raw data are even less normal (p < 0.005)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-250",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt Box-Cox transforms **ticket resolution times** (λ = 0, natural log) and runs capability with the original **USL = 48 h**. The report shows **Ppk = 6.9** for a process that misses the SLA every week. What is the MOST likely error?",options:["λ = 0 is not allowed for time data","The USL was not transformed; on the log scale it should be ln 48 ≈ 3.87","The data should have been squared instead","Ppk cannot be calculated after a transformation"],answer:1,hint:"Book rule 4: transform every spec limit and target with the same λ as the data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"After a transform the data live on a new scale. Spec limits left on the old scale give nonsense capability values."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'ln(resolution time) is roughly 1–4, while the "USL" of 48 is far above every value, so Ppk explodes. The correct limit is $\\ln 48\\approx 3.87$.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"λ = 0 (log) is the most common choice for times. Squaring makes right skew worse. Ppk on transformed data is standard practice in Minitab."}],final:"Untransformed USL → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The log (λ = 0) is one of the most common transforms for time data.","","Squaring (λ = 2) would make right-skewed time data even more skewed.","Capability on transformed data is valid as long as the specs are transformed too."],trap:"Rule 4: transform the specs with the same λ.",bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-251",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"**Page-load time** falls quickly as **server memory** rises from 2 to 8 GB, then barely changes from 16 to 64 GB. A straight line fits poorly. Apart from a polynomial model, which option does the book give?",options:["Remove all the points above 16 GB","Transform X (for example ln of memory) and fit a linear regression on the transformed data","Use a chi-square test instead of regression","Fit the linear model on the raw data and ignore the curve, because R-sq is above 50%"],answer:1,hint:"Non-linear data → either non-linear regression or linear regression on transformed X or Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book gives two routes for a curved relationship: non-linear (polynomial) regression, or **linear regression on transformed data**, where X or Y may be transformed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A fast fall followed by levelling off (diminishing returns) is often straightened by ln(X): equal doublings of memory then become equal steps. Fit Y on ln(memory), check the residuals, and report in GB."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting data hides the shape. Chi-square is for categorical data. Ignoring visible curvature leaves patterned residuals."}],final:"Transform X and fit a line → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Deleting valid data to force a straight line hides the real relationship.","","Chi-square compares categorical variables; both variables here are continuous.","A curved pattern left in the residuals breaks the model’s assumptions, whatever the R-sq."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-252",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After a square-root transformation, a Black Belt tells the steering committee that the new process mean is **“5.2 √minutes”**. The committee is confused. What should she do?",options:["Report the λ value instead","Switch to a reciprocal transform, which has clearer units","Back-transform the result into original units (5.2² ≈ 27 min) when reporting","Add 5.2 to the old mean"],answer:2,hint:"Book disadvantage of transforms: units are hard to understand, so results must be back-transformed for reporting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists hard-to-understand units as a disadvantage of transforming data. Results are back-transformed for explanation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Undo the square root by squaring: $5.2^2=27.04\\approx 27$ minutes. Present that, and note that a back-transformed centre is a median-like value, not the arithmetic mean."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"λ means nothing to a steering committee. A reciprocal has even less intuitive units. Adding numbers across scales is meaningless."}],final:"Back-transform to minutes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["λ is a technical setting and does not tell the committee the process result.","1 ÷ minutes is even harder to explain than √minutes.","","Values on different scales cannot be added together."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-253",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An I-MR chart of **daily restore times** (strongly right-skewed, all positive) shows frequent points above the UCL but none ever near the LCL. Investigations find no special causes. What is the BEST response?",options:["Widen the limits to ±4 sigma","Remove all the high points and recalculate the limits","Switch to a p chart","Use a Box-Cox transformation (Minitab offers it under Stat > Control Charts) so the I chart limits suit the data, then re-plot"],answer:3,hint:"Individuals charts assume roughly normal data; for skewed positive data, transform (Box-Cox) before charting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"I chart limits are mean ± 3 sigma, which assumes a roughly symmetric (normal) distribution. A long right tail produces false alarms above the UCL."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The process is stable but skewed. Box-Cox (found under Stat > Control Charts, as the book shows) finds λ. Chart the transformed data, or use the Box-Cox option in the I-MR chart dialog."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Arbitrary wider limits also hide real signals. Deleting points hides the real distribution. A p chart is for proportions defective, not continuous times."}],final:"Box-Cox the skewed data before charting → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Arbitrary wider limits would also hide real special causes; the problem is the shape of the data.","Deleting valid high points hides the real distribution and gives misleading limits.","A p chart is for the proportion of defective units, not for continuous times.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-254",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, which **two** things can transforming data achieve in regression work?",options:["Make non-normal data look normal, and turn a curved relationship into a straight line","Remove outliers and raise R-sq","Prove cause and effect and remove multicollinearity","Convert continuous data into attribute data and reduce the sample size needed"],answer:0,hint:"Transform → (1) normality for tools that need it, (2) linearity so that simple linear regression can be used.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s advantages of transforming are that linear regression becomes easier to see and manage, and non-normal data can be made to look normal."},{title:"Apply",body:"So a transform can straighten a curve (for linear regression) and make skewed data normal (for capability, t-tests, control charts and residual assumptions)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Transforming does not remove points, prove cause, fix correlated X’s or change the data type."}],final:"Normality and linearity → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A transform keeps every data point; it is not a way to remove outliers or to raise R-sq on purpose.","Cause and effect need a DOE, and collinearity needs a change of X’s; a transform does neither.","A transform keeps data continuous and does not change sample size needs."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-255",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why do square roots and logarithms reduce **right** skew?",options:["They shrink large values much more than small ones, pulling in the long right tail","They add a constant to every value","They remove the largest values","They stretch large values more than small ones"],answer:0,hint:"λ < 1 compresses large values more than small ones; λ > 1 stretches them.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says skewed data become more symmetric with a function that acts **more strongly on large numbers** than on small ones."},{title:"Apply",body:"For example $\\sqrt{4}=2$ but $\\sqrt{100}=10$: the 96-unit gap shrinks to 8. The long right tail is pulled in, while small values change little."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Adding a constant shifts the data without changing their shape. No values are removed. Stretching large values (λ > 1) is what fixes left skew."}],final:"Compress large values → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Adding a constant moves every value equally and leaves the skew unchanged.","A transform keeps every value; it changes their scale, not how many there are.","Stretching large values (λ > 1, squares and cubes) increases right skew; it is used for left skew."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-256",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Box-Cox estimates **λ = 0.41** (95% CI 0.12 to 0.69) and Minitab suggests a **rounded value of 0.5**. Why use 0.5 rather than 0.41?",options:["The rounded value always gives the smallest standard deviation","0.41 is not allowed, because λ must be a whole number","Rounding guarantees that the transformed data are normal","0.5 lies inside the CI and gives a simple, interpretable transform (square root) that works almost as well"],answer:3,hint:"Use a convenient λ inside the confidence interval; any value there is statistically as good as the estimate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The estimate 0.41 is just the best point on the Box-Cox curve. Any λ inside its 95% CI fits about as well, so Minitab rounds to a standard, easy-to-explain power."},{title:"Apply",body:"0.5 lies within 0.12–0.69, and λ = 0.5 is the square root, which is simple to calculate and explain. Normality is still confirmed afterwards with a test."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The exact estimate, not the rounded one, gives the minimum standard deviation. λ can be any real number. Nothing guarantees normality without a check."}],final:"Convenient λ inside the CI → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The exact estimate (0.41) gives the minimum; the rounded value is chosen for convenience, not because it is lower.","λ can be any real value; Minitab rounds only for convenience.","Normality must still be confirmed with a test after any transform.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-257",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A regression of **ln(repair time)** on **cable age (years)** gives a coefficient of **0.05** for age. How is it best interpreted in original units?",options:["Each extra year adds 0.05 hours to repair time","Each extra year multiplies repair time by about e^0.05 ≈ 1.05, roughly a 5% increase","Each extra year adds 5 hours to repair time","Repair time rises by 0.05% per year"],answer:1,hint:"With ln(Y) as the response, a coefficient b means Y is multiplied by e^b per unit of X (about 100·b % for small b).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When Y is log-transformed, adding b on the log scale means **multiplying** Y by e^b on the original scale. This is why transformed results must be back-transformed with care."},{title:"Apply",body:"$e^{0.05}\\approx 1.051$, so each extra year of cable age raises repair time by about 5%, whatever the starting level."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reading 0.05 as hours ignores the log scale. 5 hours and 0.05% misread the size of the effect."}],final:"About ×1.05 (5%) per year → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Treated the coefficient as if Y were not transformed; on the log scale effects are multiplicative.","","Confused 5% with 5 hours; the effect is a percentage of the current repair time.","Missed a factor of 100: a log coefficient of 0.05 is about 5%, not 0.05%."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-258",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"After a Box-Cox transformation, the transformed data should be checked with a **normality test** before they are used for capability or hypothesis testing. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Transform → re-test normality → then analyse.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Box-Cox picks the best power, but "best" is not the same as "normal". The book confirms its √ transform with an Anderson-Darling test (p < 0.005 before, 0.687 after).'},{title:"Apply",body:"Always run a normality test on the transformed data before relying on normal-based tools, so the statement is true."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Box-Cox gives the best power, not a guarantee of normality; the book re-tests after transforming."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-259",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"A Box-Cox transformation is a reasonable option for all of the following **EXCEPT**:",options:["Right-skewed, all-positive cycle times before a capability study","A skewed, positive process variable on an individuals control chart","Pass/fail inspection results recorded as 0 and 1","A regression whose residuals fan out as the fitted values grow"],answer:2,hint:"Box-Cox needs continuous, strictly positive data; it cannot help binary (0/1) attribute data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Box-Cox applies a power to **positive continuous** values to change the shape of the distribution or stabilise the variance."},{title:"Apply to each option",body:"Skewed cycle times, skewed values on an I chart and a residual funnel (fixed by transforming Y) are all standard uses. Pass/fail data are attribute data, and the zeros cannot even be transformed."},{title:"Pick the exception",body:"The pass/fail option: use attribute tools (proportions, p charts) instead."}],final:"Binary data → not Box-Cox → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Skewed positive cycle times before capability are a classic Box-Cox use.","Box-Cox is offered under Stat > Control Charts for skewed data.","","Transforming Y often stabilises a residual funnel."],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"},{id:"sv-4-260",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`A Black Belt straightens a curved relationship by transforming X. Put these steps in the correct order:

- **P** — Create ln(X) with Calc > Calculator
- **Q** — See curvature in the scatter plot of Y against X
- **R** — Fit the linear regression of Y on ln(X) and check its residuals
- **S** — Report the settings and predictions in original units`,options:["P → Q → R → S","Q → R → P → S","Q → P → S → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:3,hint:"See the problem → transform → fit and check → back-transform for the report.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A transform is a response to something seen in the data. The model on the new scale must pass its residual checks before results are translated back."},{title:"Order the steps",body:"**Q** see the curve → **P** create ln(X) → **R** fit and check the residuals → **S** report in original units."}],final:"Q → P → R → S → answer **D**",whyWrong:["There is no reason to transform before seeing curvature in the data.","The regression on ln(X) cannot be fitted before the ln(X) column has been created.","Results should be reported only after the residuals of the transformed model have been checked.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 459–462"}];export{e as items};
